# 17. The big picture

You can think about backend communication as three major styles:

```text
1. Direct synchronous communication

A ─────────HTTP────────> B


2. Asynchronous messaging

A ──────> MQ ──────> B


3. Event broadcasting

              ┌──> B
              │
A ──────> Topic ──> C
              │
              └──> D
```

And this leads naturally into **event-driven architecture**:

```text
                    Event
                      │
                      ▼
              ┌──────────────┐
              │ Message      │
              │ Broker       │
              └──────┬───────┘
                     │
       ┌─────────────┼──────────────┐
       ▼             ▼              ▼
   Service A     Service B      Service C
```

That's why **message queues are an important bridge between ordinary Spring Boot development and modern distributed/microservice backend development**.

If you're building your Java backend learning path, I'd learn **RabbitMQ first for the basic MQ concepts**, then **Kafka** for the more advanced event-streaming/distributed-systems model.
